@meta
id: 28-13
title: Per-filetype settings without autocommands
chapter: 28
type: lesson
skills: vimrc-filetype
par: 264
minutes: 7
vscode: "[typescript]": { ... } blocks in settings.json
open: .vimrc after/ftplugin/typescript.vim

@brief
Chapter 27 showed you the autocommand way to make a setting apply to one
language:

    autocmd FileType typescript setlocal shiftwidth=2

It works, and three languages later your vimrc has twenty of those lines
interleaved with everything else. Vim has a better place for them, and it
has had it for thirty years.

Anything in `~/.vim/after/ftplugin/<filetype>.vim` is sourced every time
a buffer of that filetype is loaded — after vim's own ftplugin for that
language, which is what `after/` means and why it is the directory you
want. Your line wins over the one that ships with vim, without you having
to argue with it.

Two jobs here.

  1. In the vimrc, replace the three `autocmd FileType typescript` lines
     with one comment saying where those settings now live:

         " per-filetype settings live in ~/.vim/after/ftplugin/<filetype>.vim

  2. In the second buffer — an empty `after/ftplugin/typescript.vim` —
     write them properly:

         " TypeScript: Prettier's defaults, and a column to aim at
         setlocal shiftwidth=2
         setlocal colorcolumn=100
         setlocal suffixesadd=.ts

`setlocal`, not `set`. These files run for one buffer, and `set` would
change the global value for every other file you have open.

@keys
| `setlocal` | set an option for this buffer only |
| `:set filetype?` | what vim thinks this buffer is |
| `dd` | delete a line |
| `:wqa` | write everything and quit |

@hints
Write the replacement comment before you delete the autocommands — `O`
from an `autocmd` line gives you a clean line, where `o` from a comment
line would start you off with another `"`.
--
The three autocommands are contiguous, so `3dd` takes all three.
--
In the empty buffer, `i` and the three `setlocal` lines, then `gg` and
`O` for the comment above them. Then `:wqa`.

@solution
    /autocmd<Enter>
    O " per-filetype settings live in ~/.vim/after/ftplugin/<filetype>.vim <Esc>
    j 3dd :w<Enter> :bn<Enter>
    i setlocal shiftwidth=2<Enter> setlocal colorcolumn=100<Enter>
    setlocal suffixesadd=.ts <Esc>
    gg O " TypeScript: Prettier's defaults, and a column to aim at <Esc>
    :wqa<Enter>

Why this is better than the autocommands it replaces, beyond tidiness:

- The file is named after the filetype, so there is exactly one place to
  look when TypeScript behaves oddly, and `:scriptnames` shows it was
  loaded.
- It cannot fire twice. Source your vimrc a second time and every
  `autocmd` in it that is not wrapped in an `augroup`/`autocmd!` pair is
  now registered twice over. An ftplugin has no such failure mode.
- It is per buffer by construction. `setlocal` in an autocommand is the
  same idea, but nothing stops you writing `set` by mistake, and the
  symptom — one file's settings leaking into another — is horrible to
  track down.

`suffixesadd=.ts` is a small gift for later: it lets `gf` open
`./order.repository` under the cursor as `order.repository.ts`.

And the reason it is `after/ftplugin` rather than `ftplugin`: vim loads
`$VIMRUNTIME/ftplugin/typescript.vim` first, and `~/.vim/ftplugin/` would
*replace* it rather than add to it. `after/` runs last, so you override
exactly what you meant to and inherit the rest.

@start .vimrc
" ~/.vimrc - one line at a time, when something annoys you.

set nocompatible
syntax on
filetype plugin indent on

" --- Filetypes ---
autocmd FileType typescript setlocal shiftwidth=2
autocmd FileType typescript setlocal colorcolumn=100
autocmd FileType typescript setlocal suffixesadd=.ts

@expected .vimrc
" ~/.vimrc - one line at a time, when something annoys you.

set nocompatible
syntax on
filetype plugin indent on

" --- Filetypes ---
" per-filetype settings live in ~/.vim/after/ftplugin/<filetype>.vim

@expected after/ftplugin/typescript.vim
" TypeScript: Prettier's defaults, and a column to aim at
setlocal shiftwidth=2
setlocal colorcolumn=100
setlocal suffixesadd=.ts
